1
00:00:05,420 --> 00:00:06,890
Morning.

2
00:00:06,890 --> 00:00:08,400
Morning.

3
00:00:09,110 --> 00:00:09,970
Sleep okay?

4
00:00:09,970 --> 00:00:11,540
Mm-hmm. Great.

5
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:15,310
Hey, can I ask you something?

6
00:00:15,310 --> 00:00:16,630
Sure.

7
00:00:16,630 --> 00:00:18,390
Last night, me wanting to try that stuff out

8
00:00:18,390 --> 00:00:19,850
of the Kama Sutra,

9
00:00:19,850 --> 00:00:22,910
was that fun for you or kinda racially insensitive?

10
00:00:23,470 --> 00:00:26,110
Yeah, just because you're in bed with an Indian woman,

11
00:00:26,110 --> 00:00:27,580
you think that gives you permission

12
00:00:27,580 --> 00:00:31,470
to use crazy positions from an ancient Indian love manual?

13
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:33,090
Hey, if you can find a book called

14
00:00:33,090 --> 00:00:35,950
Weird Sex With White Boys, I'd be okay with that.

15
00:00:38,070 --> 00:00:41,620
No, no, you have such beautiful eyes.

16
00:00:41,620 --> 00:00:43,120
Have you ever thought about getting contacts?

17
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:44,600
I tried in the seventh grade.

18
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:46,000
I could never get used to them.

19
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:47,250
Oh, that's too bad.

20
00:00:47,250 --> 00:00:49,290
Yeah, if I had contacts I would have been

21
00:00:49,290 --> 00:00:50,920
the coolest debate club president

22
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:53,750
ever to be stuffed into his own cello case.

23
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:56,990
If you had them on now,

24
00:00:56,990 --> 00:00:59,630
you could see what we're going to do next.

25
00:01:02,090 --> 00:01:05,440
Th-that's okay. I can infer from context.

26
00:01:07,570 --> 00:01:09,480
Hey, Raj, wanna see a new magic trick I've been working on?

27
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:12,260
Howard, if I may interject here

28
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:13,920
with a piece of friendly advice.

29
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:16,010
Is working on magic tricks

30
00:01:16,010 --> 00:01:18,260
really how you want to spend your time?

31
00:01:18,260 --> 00:01:21,770
Granted, you're just an engineer, but...

32
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:26,010
that doesn't mean you might not someday build a geegaw

33
00:01:26,010 --> 00:01:28,980
or a thingamabob that may get you a "thank you"

34
00:01:28,980 --> 00:01:32,560
in someone else's Nobel Prize acceptance speech.

35
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:38,910
Is the trick making him disappear?

36
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:42,460
Sure, let's see it.

37
00:01:43,490 --> 00:01:45,380
Here, shuffle these.

38
00:01:45,380 --> 00:01:47,220
Okay.

39
00:01:47,680 --> 00:01:50,170
Now spread them out on the table face down.

40
00:01:50,170 --> 00:01:51,880
Pick one, look at it.

41
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:52,820
Okay.

42
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:54,890
Now remember your card, put it back in the deck.

43
00:01:54,890 --> 00:01:57,450
Is it any wonder he doesn't have a doctorate?

44
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:00,680
Remind me, what's your birthday?

45
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:02,180
October sixth.

46
00:02:02,180 --> 00:02:05,330
Okay, October is the tenth month.

47
00:02:05,330 --> 00:02:09,790
So ten-- one plus zero is one, plus six is seven.

48
00:02:09,790 --> 00:02:11,410
Turn over the seventh card.

49
00:02:15,050 --> 00:02:16,100
How about that!

50
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:17,080
Is that your card?

51
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:19,180
Yes, it is. Very cool!

52
00:02:20,910 --> 00:02:22,390
It's not cool.

53
00:02:22,390 --> 00:02:24,640
It's a childish trick designed

54
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:27,800
to confuse and intrigue simpletons.

55
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:29,560
How'd you do it?

56
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:32,590
A magician never reveals his secrets.

57
00:02:32,590 --> 00:02:35,940
But surely a future Nobel Prize winner can figure it out.

58
00:02:37,750 --> 00:02:38,780
Fine.

59
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:40,540
Give me a second.

60
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:47,650
You ever notice when he thinks real hard, it smells like bacon?

61
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:52,640
- Oh, hey! - Sorry! Oop!

62
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:54,550
Sorry! Pardon me!

63
00:02:54,550 --> 00:02:56,180
Oh, I'm so sorry!

64
00:02:56,180 --> 00:02:58,170
Oh, that was my fault.

65
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:01,330
Check it out.

66
00:03:01,330 --> 00:03:03,520
I just got contacts.

67
00:03:31,860 --> 00:03:33,800
One, two,

68
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:36,080
three, four, five.

69
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:37,190
No way.

70
00:03:37,190 --> 00:03:38,040
That's your card, right?

71
00:03:38,040 --> 00:03:39,320
Seven of clubs!

72
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:41,650
That is amazing!

73
00:03:41,650 --> 00:03:44,150
It's not amazing.

74
00:03:45,710 --> 00:03:48,170
All magic tricks are merely a combination

75
00:03:48,170 --> 00:03:50,400
of manipulation and misdirection

76
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:53,830
with the occasional dwarf in a box wiggling his feet.

77
00:03:55,170 --> 00:03:57,020
Oh, really? So how did I do it?

78
00:03:57,020 --> 00:03:59,360
I don't care how you did it.

79
00:03:59,850 --> 00:04:02,410
I have more important things to think about.

80
00:04:06,460 --> 00:04:08,770
Clearly, the cards are marked.

81
00:04:09,470 --> 00:04:11,730
I thought you had more important things to think about.

82
00:04:11,730 --> 00:04:13,330
I do. You just happened

83
00:04:13,330 --> 00:04:15,320
to have caught me on a break.

84
00:04:17,530 --> 00:04:19,240
Oh, let me see those cards.

85
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:21,780
Big deal. Not knowing

86
00:04:21,780 --> 00:04:22,880
is part of the fun.

87
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:23,910
"Not knowing is part of the fun."

88
00:04:23,910 --> 00:04:26,350
Was that the motto of your community college?

89
00:04:29,280 --> 00:04:31,100
Hey, guys, what do you think?

90
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:32,570
Priya took me clothes shopping.

91
00:04:32,570 --> 00:04:34,060
Hey, you look great.

92
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:34,960
Good for you, Priya.

93
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:36,470
I could never get that stupid hoodie off him

94
00:04:36,470 --> 00:04:37,800
when we were...

95
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:39,240
Well, you look great.

96
00:04:40,140 --> 00:04:41,030
Thank you.

97
00:04:41,030 --> 00:04:42,130
Let's go hang everything up.

98
00:04:42,130 --> 00:04:43,610
Yeah, good.

99
00:04:46,010 --> 00:04:46,970
New contacts.

100
00:04:46,970 --> 00:04:49,150
I should have done this years ago.

101
00:04:52,190 --> 00:04:54,960
So you finally getting used to them doing it on a daily basis?

102
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:55,860
I'm not going out with him.

103
00:04:55,860 --> 00:04:57,500
He can sleep with whoever he wants.

104
00:04:57,500 --> 00:04:59,940
Yeah, I was talking to Raj.

105
00:05:04,960 --> 00:05:06,820
Oopsy.

106
00:05:06,820 --> 00:05:08,820
This deck is rigged in some fashion.

107
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:09,830
Fine. Get another deck

108
00:05:09,830 --> 00:05:10,790
and I'll do the trick with that.

109
00:05:10,790 --> 00:05:12,240
So you're saying this is a regulation deck?

110
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:14,660
I'm saying believe in magic, you muggle.

111
00:05:19,330 --> 00:05:22,100
You know, well, I'm happy Leonard's found someone.

112
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:26,090
Not that anybody asked.

113
00:05:30,630 --> 00:05:33,710
How many Star Trek uniforms do you have?

114
00:05:34,730 --> 00:05:35,840
Just two:

115
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:37,420
everyday and dress.

116
00:05:40,620 --> 00:05:43,460
Listen, we need to talk about something.

117
00:05:43,460 --> 00:05:45,500
Oh, my God, you're breaking up with me!

118
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:46,830
Why would you take me out shopping

119
00:05:46,830 --> 00:05:49,610
and then break up with me? That is so cruel!

120
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:53,090
Leonard, I'm not breaking up with you.

121
00:05:53,090 --> 00:05:54,480
Oh, okay.

122
00:05:58,590 --> 00:06:00,270
So, what's up?

123
00:06:01,640 --> 00:06:03,330
It's a little weird,

124
00:06:03,330 --> 00:06:06,340
your ex-girlfriend hanging out here all the time.

125
00:06:06,340 --> 00:06:06,940
I know.

126
00:06:06,940 --> 00:06:08,770
I usually never see my ex-girlfriends,

127
00:06:08,770 --> 00:06:10,870
unless their hard drives crash.

128
00:06:12,990 --> 00:06:14,900
I'm just saying, how would you feel

129
00:06:14,900 --> 00:06:17,320
if I was hanging out with my ex-boyfriend?

130
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:18,770
Um, not great, I guess.

131
00:06:18,770 --> 00:06:20,410
But Penny and I are just friends.

132
00:06:20,410 --> 00:06:21,510
I don't care.

133
00:06:21,510 --> 00:06:23,690
This is a woman you have slept with.

134
00:06:23,690 --> 00:06:24,800
If you want her around,

135
00:06:24,800 --> 00:06:28,200
then I have to wonder if maybe you're not ready to move on.

136
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:30,050
No, no-no-no, I'm ready!

137
00:06:30,050 --> 00:06:31,890
I gave up the gift of sight for you.

138
00:06:31,890 --> 00:06:33,930
If that's not moving on, what is?

139
00:06:34,990 --> 00:06:37,870
You need to cut the cord with Penny.

140
00:06:37,870 --> 00:06:39,920
Oh... okay.

141
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:41,910
You don't sound very confident.

142
00:06:41,910 --> 00:06:43,750
No, no, I'm confident.

143
00:06:43,750 --> 00:06:46,570
Its not like I'm unfamiliar with the "get lost" conversation.

144
00:06:46,570 --> 00:06:49,530
Although, this will be the first time I'll be the one saying it.

145
00:06:50,370 --> 00:06:51,550
Thank you.

146
00:06:51,550 --> 00:06:53,920
- I appreciate it. - Sure.

147
00:06:54,410 --> 00:06:57,090
Oh, God, you smell so good.

148
00:06:57,090 --> 00:06:58,050
Of course, that could just be

149
00:06:58,050 --> 00:07:00,370
my other senses getting stronger.

150
00:07:04,780 --> 00:07:07,720
Hypothetically, if I had access

151
00:07:07,720 --> 00:07:12,250
to a Lieutenant Uhura uniform, would you wear it?

152
00:07:12,970 --> 00:07:15,320
Leonard, it's a source of great pain to me and my family

153
00:07:15,320 --> 00:07:17,360
that my brother has that outfit in his wardrobe.

154
00:07:17,360 --> 00:07:19,150
Can we not discuss it?

155
00:07:20,100 --> 00:07:21,900
I'm sorry. Never mind.

156
00:07:26,230 --> 00:07:28,240
And was your card the jack of diamonds?

157
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:31,910
Oh, unbelievable!

158
00:07:35,730 --> 00:07:37,540
Know how he did it yet?

159
00:07:44,500 --> 00:07:46,910
Aw, he has the same look my little nephew gets

160
00:07:46,910 --> 00:07:49,980
when he can't figure out how I got his nose.

161
00:07:52,900 --> 00:07:55,040
You know, I have some remedial magic tricks

162
00:07:55,040 --> 00:07:56,620
that might be more your speed.

163
00:07:56,620 --> 00:07:58,660
Like, ooh, look!

164
00:08:00,020 --> 00:08:01,820
The pencil's rubber!

165
00:08:03,550 --> 00:08:06,060
Hey, I think I know how you did the card trick.

166
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:09,250
Oh, please, if I don't know, you don't know.

167
00:08:09,250 --> 00:08:11,400
That's axiomatic.

168
00:08:11,400 --> 00:08:12,570
Come here.

169
00:08:15,030 --> 00:08:16,670
You're right.

170
00:08:17,770 --> 00:08:18,990
Not too bad for someone

171
00:08:18,990 --> 00:08:22,300
who doesn't know what "axiomatic" means.

172
00:08:32,210 --> 00:08:33,520
What happened with the contacts?

173
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:34,780
One of them's upstairs.

174
00:08:34,780 --> 00:08:37,440
The other one sort of slipped back into my skull.

175
00:08:38,950 --> 00:08:40,860
Hey, is that one of the new shirts Priya got you?

176
00:08:40,860 --> 00:08:42,150
Yeah.

177
00:08:42,150 --> 00:08:43,820
You don't wanna wash that.

178
00:08:43,820 --> 00:08:45,680
- No? - No, that's silk.

179
00:08:45,680 --> 00:08:48,360
Seriously, what would you do without me?

180
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:49,930
Yeah.

181
00:08:51,960 --> 00:08:53,170
You know, I gotta tell you, I think

182
00:08:53,170 --> 00:08:56,290
- you got a real winner with Priya. - Yeah, me, too.

183
00:08:56,290 --> 00:08:57,660
She seems really sweet.

184
00:08:57,660 --> 00:08:59,760
Believe me, you do not want to take that for granted.

185
00:08:59,760 --> 00:09:01,700
There are a lot of bitches out there.

186
00:09:03,740 --> 00:09:06,120
Yeah, bitches are the worst.

187
00:09:07,460 --> 00:09:08,750
All right, I'll see you later.

188
00:09:08,750 --> 00:09:11,570
Uh, Penny, there's something I have to tell you.

189
00:09:11,570 --> 00:09:13,230
What?

190
00:09:13,230 --> 00:09:15,330
Okay, uh, how do I put this?

191
00:09:15,330 --> 00:09:19,960
Um... are you familiar with Darwin's observation

192
00:09:19,960 --> 00:09:22,580
of the finches in the Galapagos Islands?

193
00:09:23,700 --> 00:09:26,330
Did they make a movie about it?

194
00:09:26,330 --> 00:09:26,930
No.

195
00:09:26,930 --> 00:09:28,240
Then no.

196
00:09:29,050 --> 00:09:32,750
All right, well, anyway, Darwin observed that when two groups

197
00:09:32,750 --> 00:09:35,050
of finches competed over the same food source,

198
00:09:35,050 --> 00:09:38,410
eventually one of them would evolve a different beak shape

199
00:09:38,410 --> 00:09:41,680
so they could feed on something else.

200
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:43,050
Okay.

201
00:09:44,040 --> 00:09:46,850
So what do you think we can learn from that behavior

202
00:09:46,850 --> 00:09:49,880
that we can apply to our own lives?

203
00:09:50,670 --> 00:09:54,510
Uh, a bird in the hand is worth two in the bush?

204
00:09:57,090 --> 00:09:59,020
Sure, that has birds in it.

205
00:10:01,870 --> 00:10:05,330
It's fun talking to you, Leonard; I always learn stuff.

206
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:11,200
I'm sorry, but I'm gonna count that.

207
00:10:14,990 --> 00:10:18,010
Do you really have to wear that in bed?

208
00:10:21,030 --> 00:10:24,620
Yeah. The doctor didn't want me to rub my eye during the night.

209
00:10:24,620 --> 00:10:27,320
It was either this or one of those giant neck cones.

210
00:10:29,550 --> 00:10:32,630
So did you get to talk to Penny?

211
00:10:32,630 --> 00:10:34,890
Oh, yes. Indeed, I did.

212
00:10:36,470 --> 00:10:37,180
And?

213
00:10:37,180 --> 00:10:38,810
She completely understood everything I said.

214
00:10:38,810 --> 00:10:40,630
Case closed.

215
00:10:40,630 --> 00:10:42,820
Was she upset?

216
00:10:42,820 --> 00:10:45,910
Maybe, but that's not my problem.

217
00:10:45,910 --> 00:10:48,510
She's not my girlfriend, you are.

218
00:10:48,510 --> 00:10:50,630
I hope you weren't cruel to her.

219
00:10:51,890 --> 00:10:54,890
Is the autumn cruel for letting the flowers die,

220
00:10:54,890 --> 00:10:56,950
or is that just nature's way?

221
00:11:00,810 --> 00:11:04,620
Leonard?  Leonard?  Leonard?

222
00:11:04,620 --> 00:11:05,400
What?!

223
00:11:05,400 --> 00:11:08,690
I think I figured out Wolowitz's magic trick,

224
00:11:09,710 --> 00:11:11,890
and I need you to pick a card.

225
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,970
I am not opening that door, Sheldon!

226
00:11:15,970 --> 00:11:17,580
As you wish.

227
00:11:22,710 --> 00:11:24,630
Pick a card, put it back,

228
00:11:24,630 --> 00:11:26,960
and prepare to be amazed.

229
00:11:28,610 --> 00:11:30,380
Did you pick one?

230
00:11:30,650 --> 00:11:31,900
Yep.

231
00:11:34,180 --> 00:11:36,960
Is this your card?

232
00:11:38,010 --> 00:11:38,730
Nope.

233
00:11:38,730 --> 00:11:40,490
Drat.

234
00:11:41,650 --> 00:11:43,710
Is this your card?

235
00:11:44,700 --> 00:11:46,140
No.

236
00:11:46,730 --> 00:11:48,310
I'll be right back.

237
00:11:56,390 --> 00:11:58,370
Pick a card, Sheldon.

238
00:12:00,210 --> 00:12:03,170
Now look at it, and put it back in the deck.

239
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:06,660
Now do you remember your card?

240
00:12:06,660 --> 00:12:09,410
Of course I do, I have an eidetic memory.

241
00:12:09,930 --> 00:12:11,150
My apologies.

242
00:12:11,150 --> 00:12:13,020
Now shuffle the cards.

243
00:12:13,520 --> 00:12:15,530
Shuffling.

244
00:12:16,780 --> 00:12:18,220
Wait here.

245
00:12:20,010 --> 00:12:23,290
Processing image, cross-referencing...

246
00:12:23,290 --> 00:12:24,230
Whatcha doin'?

247
00:12:24,230 --> 00:12:27,570
I'm reverse engineering Wolowitz's magic trick.

248
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:31,340
What's up with the infrared cameras?

249
00:12:31,340 --> 00:12:34,220
I'm measuring residual heat levels on the playing cards

250
00:12:34,220 --> 00:12:36,490
to determine which one's been touched.

251
00:12:36,930 --> 00:12:37,470
By the way,

252
00:12:37,470 --> 00:12:38,530
if you hope to have children,

253
00:12:38,530 --> 00:12:40,940
I suggest you switch from briefs to boxers.

254
00:12:40,940 --> 00:12:43,380
Your testicles look a tad warm.

255
00:12:52,310 --> 00:12:54,060
Holy crap, are you connected

256
00:12:54,060 --> 00:12:56,200
to the Oak Ridge National Laboratory?

257
00:12:56,200 --> 00:12:58,620
Yes. I'm using their Cray supercomputer

258
00:12:58,620 --> 00:13:00,790
to analyze shuffling patterns.

259
00:13:01,490 --> 00:13:03,940
Sheldon, that computer is used for national defense.

260
00:13:03,940 --> 00:13:05,860
Hacking into it is a federal crime.

261
00:13:05,860 --> 00:13:08,710
Relax, we're not under attack right now.

262
00:13:11,190 --> 00:13:14,720
Okay, I'm leaving before the black helicopters get here.

263
00:13:15,770 --> 00:13:19,330
And is this my card?

264
00:13:19,890 --> 00:13:21,630
Rats!

265
00:13:24,810 --> 00:13:28,600
I wonder if Howard used a radioactive tracer.

266
00:13:28,600 --> 00:13:32,390
Where am I going to find uranium-235 this time of night?

267
00:13:35,290 --> 00:13:37,240
Come on, CraigsList.

268
00:13:41,930 --> 00:13:45,020
Is it racist that I took you to an Indian restaurant?

269
00:13:45,890 --> 00:13:47,750
It's okay, I like Indian food.

270
00:13:47,750 --> 00:13:50,970
Or as you probably call it back home, "food."

271
00:13:53,340 --> 00:13:54,500
Why didn't you wear your contacts?

272
00:13:54,500 --> 00:13:55,350
Don't you like them?

273
00:13:55,350 --> 00:13:56,360
No, no, I do.

274
00:13:56,360 --> 00:13:58,550
It's just I knew I was having curry tonight

275
00:13:58,550 --> 00:14:02,080
and I only want one part of my body to burn at a time.

276
00:14:04,230 --> 00:14:05,440
You know...

277
00:14:06,390 --> 00:14:10,310
...my company offered me a position here in Los Angeles.

278
00:14:10,750 --> 00:14:13,620
Does that mean you'd relocate here permanently?

279
00:14:13,620 --> 00:14:16,310
Maybe. What do you think?

280
00:14:16,310 --> 00:14:17,920
I love it.

281
00:14:17,920 --> 00:14:19,830
That's great!

282
00:14:20,810 --> 00:14:22,820
Hi!

283
00:14:24,200 --> 00:14:26,690
Hi! Just hang on.

284
00:14:29,140 --> 00:14:30,120
Small world.

285
00:14:30,120 --> 00:14:31,150
You did talk to her, didn't you?

286
00:14:31,150 --> 00:14:33,780
Of course, I did. I talked and talked and talked.

287
00:14:34,520 --> 00:14:39,090
Hey! Look at Leonard in his fancy-ass grownup clothes.

288
00:14:40,620 --> 00:14:42,540
Way to go, Priya. For once, he doesn't look

289
00:14:42,540 --> 00:14:45,450
like a mannequin in the boys' department.

290
00:14:46,980 --> 00:14:47,550
Hey, did he

291
00:14:47,550 --> 00:14:49,640
tell you I saved the silk shirt?

292
00:14:49,640 --> 00:14:50,720
No, he did not.

293
00:14:50,720 --> 00:14:52,270
He was gonna throw it in the washing machine

294
00:14:52,270 --> 00:14:54,700
with his Spiderman underwear.

295
00:14:55,640 --> 00:14:57,830
That's our Lenny, huh?

296
00:14:58,090 --> 00:15:00,250
Yes, that's our Lenny.

297
00:15:00,250 --> 00:15:01,810
So how's the food here?

298
00:15:02,530 --> 00:15:05,920
Oh, wow, that is really, really good.

299
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:07,580
Oh, I'm sorry. You guys are on a date.

300
00:15:07,580 --> 00:15:09,900
We can hang out anytime. Have fun.

301
00:15:09,900 --> 00:15:11,390
Bye. Good night.

302
00:15:12,580 --> 00:15:15,460
Oh, that's, hot, hot, hot, hot!

303
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:20,750
Whoo! I really sneaks up on ya, huh?

304
00:15:20,750 --> 00:15:22,970
All right, I gotta go.

305
00:15:27,270 --> 00:15:28,900
It is pretty spicy.

306
00:15:32,220 --> 00:15:34,210
Oh, I've been meaning to tell you,

307
00:15:34,210 --> 00:15:36,640
I figured out your magic trick.

308
00:15:36,960 --> 00:15:37,960
Really?

309
00:15:37,960 --> 00:15:39,040
Oh, yes.

310
00:15:39,040 --> 00:15:41,640
In fact, I improved upon it.

311
00:15:42,180 --> 00:15:43,640
I'd love to see that.

312
00:15:43,640 --> 00:15:45,110
How about you, Raj?

313
00:15:45,110 --> 00:15:46,410
Beefaroni and a show?

314
00:15:46,410 --> 00:15:47,950
How do you turn that down?

315
00:15:49,570 --> 00:15:51,490
All right.

316
00:15:53,590 --> 00:15:54,920
Pick a card.

317
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:56,690
Okay.

318
00:15:58,100 --> 00:16:01,300
Bippity-boppity-boo.

319
00:16:10,030 --> 00:16:12,370
What's with the wand and the beep?

320
00:16:12,370 --> 00:16:14,380
The wand is called showmanship,

321
00:16:14,380 --> 00:16:16,780
and the beep is none of your business.

322
00:16:18,620 --> 00:16:19,860
Oh! Excuse me.

323
00:16:19,860 --> 00:16:21,180
I'm getting a text message

324
00:16:21,180 --> 00:16:24,370
completely unrelated to this magic trick.

325
00:16:24,870 --> 00:16:27,450
Oh, look, my dry cleaning's ready.

326
00:16:27,450 --> 00:16:30,000
And your card was the five of spades.

327
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:31,960
Ta-da.

328
00:16:32,620 --> 00:16:35,070
These cards have barcodes on them.

329
00:16:35,070 --> 00:16:36,280
The wand is a reader.

330
00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:38,370
It's transmitting to your phone.

331
00:16:43,020 --> 00:16:43,830
I said, "ta-da."

332
00:16:43,830 --> 00:16:45,670
Show's over.

333
00:16:46,050 --> 00:16:47,350
That's pathetic.

334
00:16:47,350 --> 00:16:49,980
Let me show you how a real magician does it.

335
00:16:49,980 --> 00:16:51,240
Raj, take a card.

336
00:16:51,240 --> 00:16:52,960
Don't let me see it.

337
00:16:52,960 --> 00:16:55,030
Okay.

338
00:16:55,550 --> 00:16:57,230
Three of clubs.

339
00:16:57,230 --> 00:16:58,390
Son of a gun,

340
00:16:58,390 --> 00:17:00,680
you're blowing my mind!

341
00:17:03,690 --> 00:17:06,720
Bippity-boppity-boo-yah!

342
00:17:08,490 --> 00:17:11,440
That does it. I'm getting uranium.

343
00:17:15,630 --> 00:17:16,960
You ever gonna tell him?

344
00:17:16,960 --> 00:17:19,870
Maybe. When it stops being fun.

345
00:17:20,790 --> 00:17:22,800
So never.

346
00:17:30,770 --> 00:17:32,260
Oh, hey, I was just on my way to work.

347
00:17:32,260 --> 00:17:33,550
Oh, okay, its not important.

348
00:17:33,550 --> 00:17:34,330
It can wait.

349
00:17:34,330 --> 00:17:36,040
No, it's all right. Walk me down.

350
00:17:36,410 --> 00:17:37,330
So, what's up?

351
00:17:37,330 --> 00:17:39,200
Uh, I kind of have a problem

352
00:17:39,200 --> 00:17:40,830
I was hoping you could help me with.

353
00:17:40,830 --> 00:17:42,030
Sure, anything.

354
00:17:42,030 --> 00:17:43,770
Move out.

355
00:17:44,830 --> 00:17:45,570
What?

356
00:17:45,570 --> 00:17:47,030
Not far.

357
00:17:47,030 --> 00:17:48,580
Hey, if you ever wanna start a family,

358
00:17:48,580 --> 00:17:51,380
La Canada has some great schools.

359
00:17:51,790 --> 00:17:52,770
Okay, I'm not moving anywhere.

360
00:17:52,770 --> 00:17:54,580
What the hell is this all about?

361
00:17:54,580 --> 00:17:56,960
Why does it have to be about anything?

362
00:17:56,960 --> 00:17:59,020
Can't a fella ask his buddy to relocate,

363
00:17:59,020 --> 00:18:00,930
no questions asked?

364
00:18:01,770 --> 00:18:03,150
Oh, for God's sake, Leonard,

365
00:18:03,150 --> 00:18:04,730
this is about Priya, isn't it?

366
00:18:04,730 --> 00:18:06,360
She doesn't want me hanging out with you.

367
00:18:06,360 --> 00:18:09,300
Yes. There, I said it.

368
00:18:11,260 --> 00:18:12,040
Okay, look.

369
00:18:12,040 --> 00:18:13,930
I happen to like your girlfriend.

370
00:18:13,930 --> 00:18:15,130
And she likes you.

371
00:18:15,130 --> 00:18:15,840
No, she doesn't.

372
00:18:15,840 --> 00:18:17,390
Not really, no.

373
00:18:17,940 --> 00:18:19,500
It doesn't matter. Look, I promise from now on

374
00:18:19,500 --> 00:18:21,010
I will keep my distance from you.

375
00:18:21,010 --> 00:18:22,240
Well, now, hold on.

376
00:18:22,240 --> 00:18:24,020
What kind of distance are we talking about?

377
00:18:24,020 --> 00:18:25,220
Because we are neighbors.

378
00:18:25,220 --> 00:18:28,240
I mean, I can hear the toilet flush in your apartment.

379
00:18:28,700 --> 00:18:30,220
You can hear my toilet flush?

380
00:18:30,220 --> 00:18:32,850
I don't listen for it, but it's nice to know

381
00:18:32,850 --> 00:18:34,950
everything's okay with your plumbing.

382
00:18:36,120 --> 00:18:38,090
The building's plumbing.

383
00:18:38,530 --> 00:18:39,800
Leonard, I get it.

384
00:18:39,800 --> 00:18:41,010
You're in a new relationship now.

385
00:18:41,010 --> 00:18:42,130
And I'm happy for you.

386
00:18:42,130 --> 00:18:44,270
So why don't we just shake hands and part friends?

387
00:18:44,270 --> 00:18:45,860
Well, now hold on.

388
00:18:45,860 --> 00:18:47,130
How about this?

389
00:18:47,130 --> 00:18:50,610
How about we still hang out, but on the down-low?

390
00:18:52,280 --> 00:18:54,400
Are you really that kind of guy?

391
00:18:54,400 --> 00:18:56,000
No.

392
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:59,300
I actually felt kinda silly just saying "on the down-low."

393
00:19:01,470 --> 00:19:02,980
Good-bye, Leonard.

394
00:19:06,820 --> 00:19:08,450
Penny, wait.

395
00:19:14,980 --> 00:19:17,580
Damn contacts.

396
00:19:21,820 --> 00:19:23,340
Incoming.

397
00:19:24,610 --> 00:19:26,830
- Hi! - Hey!

398
00:19:26,830 --> 00:19:28,330
Hello.

399
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:30,930
Why so glum, chum?

400
00:19:32,010 --> 00:19:35,440
Apparently, you can't hack into a government supercomputer

401
00:19:35,440 --> 00:19:37,240
and then try to buy uranium

402
00:19:37,240 --> 00:19:40,030
without the Department of Homeland Security tattling

403
00:19:40,030 --> 00:19:41,740
to your mother.

404
00:19:46,620 --> 00:19:47,790
Oh, that's too bad.

405
00:19:47,790 --> 00:19:49,040
Figure out the magic trick yet?

406
00:19:49,040 --> 00:19:51,040
Figure out the magic trick yet?

407
00:19:52,330 --> 00:19:53,590
Want me to tell you how to do it?

408
00:19:53,590 --> 00:19:54,260
No.

409
00:19:54,260 --> 00:19:55,880
I'll show you one more time.

410
00:19:56,390 --> 00:19:57,810
Raj?

411
00:19:58,360 --> 00:19:59,990
Hang on.

412
00:19:59,990 --> 00:20:01,340
This time do it with me,

413
00:20:01,340 --> 00:20:04,260
so I can make sure there's no monkey business.

414
00:20:06,640 --> 00:20:08,060
All right.

415
00:20:21,810 --> 00:20:24,180
- Two of hearts. - I hate you.

416
00:20:31,140 --> 00:20:33,660
Yeah, he's gonna win the Nobel Prize.

